Pe 2.5.0: Metrowerks-era compiler flags gcc will not accept
===========================================================

**Status: UNFINISHED. Do not treat this as a working recipe edit.** Two of the three
blockers below are fixed and proven; the third is open. Pe still does not build on arm64.
The recipe carrying the partial fix is deliberately *not* in `recipes/`, because that
directory means "as actually built" and this is not.

Why anyone cares about `pe`
---------------------------

Nothing depends on it. It was picked up only because it was the visible failure that read
as "no yacc in the build environment", and chasing that diagnosis is what uncovered the
poisoned `bison` bootstrap package described in `README.md` — which *did* matter, because
it silently breaks any port whose build runs `bison`. Pe itself is a BeOS-era application
and its build carries a stack of independent rot, each layer only visible once the one
above it is cleared.

The layers, in the order they appear
------------------------------------

1. **A poisoned `bison` (fixed — see `README.md`).** `sys-devel/jam`, which `pe`
   build-requires, runs `bison -y -d jamgram.y`. haikuporter was resolving `cmd:bison`
   to `bison-3.8.2_bootstrap-1-arm64.hpkg`, whose `bin/bison` is an **x86-64 Linux**
   executable. Once that package is out of the way and the genuine
   `bison-3.8.2-1-arm64.hpkg` is selected, the `Yacc1` step succeeds.

2. **`jam`'s install step was x86-only (fixed — see `recipes/jam-2.5_2021_10_29.recipe`).**
   It ran `install -v bin.haikux86/g/jam`, but jam builds into `bin.$OSPLAT`, which is
   plain `bin.haiku` on an architecture its Jamfile has no `OSPLAT` spelling for. jam now
   builds: `jam-2.5_2021_10_29-3-arm64.hpkg`.

3. **Pe's own Jamfiles use `mwcc` flags (OPEN).** With jam available, `pe` reaches its own
   compile and dies on the first object:

       gcc: error: unrecognized command-line option '-prefix'; did you mean '--prefix'?

   Two flags are involved, both Metrowerks C/C++ spellings that gcc has no equivalent for
   at the same position:

   | File | Flag | Meaning |
   |---|---|---|
   | `Sources/Jamfile:12` | `-prefix $(PREFIX_FILE)` | mwcc prefix file; gcc spells this `-include <header>` |
   | `Languages/Sources/Jamfile:9` | `-prefix LanguageIncludes.h -w nounusedvar` | as above, plus an mwcc warning selector gcc reads as `-w` followed by an input filename |

   `jamfile_cache` in the source tree also carries both lines, so it has to be removed or
   the parsed cache reintroduces them.

Why the obvious substitution is not enough
------------------------------------------

Rewriting `-prefix` to `-include` is necessary but **not sufficient**, and this is the open
part. `PREFIX_FILE` is empty for at least one target, so the flag degenerates into
`-include` with no argument and silently consumes the *next* token:

       gcc -c -o objects-nodebug/Sources/HAppResFile.o ... -fno-omit-frame-pointer \
           -include -O7 -DH_BUILDING_LIBHEKKEL ...

`-O7` becomes the argument to `-include`, so the compile fails again — a different error
with the same shape. The flag therefore has to be made conditional on `PREFIX_FILE` being
non-empty rather than merely respelled, something like:

    @@ Sources/Jamfile
    -	C++FLAGS += -prefix $(PREFIX_FILE) ;
    +	if $(PREFIX_FILE) {
    +		C++FLAGS += -include $(PREFIX_FILE) ;
    +	}

(That hunk header is illustrative, not line-numbered — see `recipes/README.md` on why the
`.patch` files in this directory are explanations rather than reliable `patch(1)` inputs.)
Whether `-O7` itself survives is a separate question: it is not a gcc optimisation level
either, and gcc only tolerates it because it clamps unknown `-O<n>` upward.

Retire when
-----------

Pe builds with `PREFIX_FILE` handled conditionally and `-w nounusedvar` dropped, **and the
result has been run**, not merely packaged. Until then the honest summary is: the
*yacc* blocker named in the original report is closed and was never really about yacc,
and `pe` has its own unrelated build rot that nobody has needed badly enough to finish.
